1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,536
A rubber ball bounces back high.

2
00:00:02,536 --> 00:00:04,377
A cricket ball bounces less.

3
00:00:04,377 --> 00:00:06,521
A lump of dough just goes splat.

4
00:00:06,921 --> 00:00:08,624
Same floor, same drop.

5
00:00:08,624 --> 00:00:10,217
Very different bounces.

6
00:00:10,667 --> 00:00:14,339
Diya drops three balls from one
metre onto the kitchen floor.

7
00:00:14,339 --> 00:00:17,640
A rubber ball, a cloth
ball, and a lump of dough.

8
00:00:17,990 --> 00:00:20,957
The rubber ball comes back
to eighty one centimetres.

9
00:00:20,957 --> 00:00:22,863
The cloth ball, to thirty six.

10
00:00:22,863 --> 00:00:24,192
The dough, to zero.

11
00:00:24,192 --> 00:00:26,271
Can one number describe each bounce?

12
00:00:26,771 --> 00:00:27,671
It can.

13
00:00:27,401 --> 00:00:29,912
It is called the
coefficient of restitution.

14
00:00:30,512 --> 00:00:32,441
Restitution means giving back.

15
00:00:32,441 --> 00:00:35,184
How much speed does a collision give back?

16
00:00:35,534 --> 00:00:36,978
We compare two speeds.

17
00:00:36,978 --> 00:00:41,183
The speed of separation: how fast
the bodies move apart after the hit.

18
00:00:41,183 --> 00:00:44,543
And the speed of approach:
how fast they came together.

19
00:00:44,943 --> 00:00:49,220
e equals the speed of separation
divided by the speed of approach.

20
00:00:49,220 --> 00:00:51,755
Both are speeds, so e has no unit.

21
00:00:52,355 --> 00:00:53,676
Our motion notebook.

22
00:00:53,676 --> 00:00:56,004
The rubber ball falls from one metre.

23
00:00:56,004 --> 00:00:57,528
g is nine point eight.

24
00:00:57,878 --> 00:01:01,490
It hits the floor at four point
four three metres per second.

25
00:01:01,890 --> 00:01:04,286
It leaves at three point nine eight.

26
00:01:04,286 --> 00:01:08,748
The floor does not move, so e is
three point nine eight over four point

27
00:01:08,748 --> 00:01:09,648
four three.

28
00:01:09,641 --> 00:01:10,834
Zero point nine.

29
00:01:11,234 --> 00:01:13,372
It rises to eighty one centimetres.

30
00:01:13,372 --> 00:01:16,823
Every new bounce is lower,
by the same factor each time.

31
00:01:17,323 --> 00:01:18,312
Slow-mo replay.

32
00:01:18,312 --> 00:01:19,689
Freeze at the floor.

33
00:01:20,039 --> 00:01:22,916
Coming in, four point
four three, downward.

34
00:01:22,916 --> 00:01:25,746
Going out, three point nine eight, upward.

35
00:01:26,146 --> 00:01:29,930
The ball squashes and springs
back, but not all the way.

36
00:01:29,930 --> 00:01:33,049
A little energy stays
behind as heat and sound.

37
00:01:33,649 --> 00:01:35,003
Now the cloth ball.

38
00:01:35,003 --> 00:01:36,461
e is zero point six.

39
00:01:36,461 --> 00:01:38,973
It rises to only thirty six centimetres.

40
00:01:39,373 --> 00:01:40,433
And the dough.

41
00:01:40,433 --> 00:01:41,333
e is zero.

42
00:01:41,305 --> 00:01:42,750
It hits, and stays.

43
00:01:42,750 --> 00:01:44,241
Nothing is given back.

44
00:01:44,841 --> 00:01:47,119
Why do the heights follow e squared?

45
00:01:47,119 --> 00:01:48,115
Step by step.

46
00:01:48,465 --> 00:01:49,365
Step one.

47
00:01:49,278 --> 00:01:54,149
Falling from height h, the ball hits
the floor at v equals root two g h.

48
00:01:54,899 --> 00:01:55,799
Step two.

49
00:01:55,650 --> 00:01:57,546
It bounces off at e times v.

50
00:01:58,296 --> 00:01:59,228
Step three.

51
00:01:59,228 --> 00:02:03,533
It rises to height h one, where
e v equals root two g h one.

52
00:02:04,283 --> 00:02:05,219
Step four.

53
00:02:05,219 --> 00:02:06,488
Divide the two.

54
00:02:06,488 --> 00:02:08,887
e equals root of h one over h.

55
00:02:08,887 --> 00:02:11,502
So h one equals e squared times h.

56
00:02:12,252 --> 00:02:16,157
For the rubber ball, zero point nine
squared is zero point eight one.

57
00:02:16,157 --> 00:02:17,505
Eighty one centimetres.

58
00:02:18,105 --> 00:02:19,127
Remember this.

59
00:02:19,127 --> 00:02:22,719
e is the speed apart, divided
by the speed of approach.

60
00:02:22,719 --> 00:02:24,687
It lies between zero and one.

61
00:02:25,387 --> 00:02:27,872
Now, how does e connect
the collisions we know?

62
00:02:27,872 --> 00:02:28,772
Link by link.

63
00:02:29,051 --> 00:02:32,148
e equals one: they part
as fast as they met.

64
00:02:32,148 --> 00:02:33,761
Kinetic energy is kept.

65
00:02:33,761 --> 00:02:35,728
That is an elastic collision.

66
00:02:36,128 --> 00:02:38,692
e equals zero: they do not part at all.

67
00:02:38,692 --> 00:02:39,592
They stick.

68
00:02:39,522 --> 00:02:41,229
That is perfectly inelastic.

69
00:02:41,629 --> 00:02:45,188
Between zero and one: some
bounce, some energy lost.

70
00:02:45,188 --> 00:02:47,726
Almost every real collision lives here.

71
00:02:48,326 --> 00:02:49,449
Side by side.

72
00:02:49,449 --> 00:02:54,502
On the left, a ball hits a still wall
at ten metres per second, and comes

73
00:02:54,502 --> 00:02:55,625
back at five.

74
00:02:55,625 --> 00:02:57,933
e is five over ten, one half.

75
00:02:58,333 --> 00:03:01,630
On the right, cart A at
six chases cart B at two.

76
00:03:01,630 --> 00:03:03,175
They close in at four.

77
00:03:03,175 --> 00:03:06,032
After the hit, A has three and B has five.

78
00:03:06,032 --> 00:03:07,287
They part at two.

79
00:03:07,287 --> 00:03:09,704
e is two over four, one half again.

80
00:03:10,304 --> 00:03:11,288
Now you try.

81
00:03:11,288 --> 00:03:16,127
A ball hits the floor, and rises to
one quarter of the height it fell from.

82
00:03:16,127 --> 00:03:18,297
Is e one quarter, or one half?

83
00:03:18,297 --> 00:03:19,521
Pause and decide.

84
00:03:22,721 --> 00:03:23,621
One half!

85
00:03:23,462 --> 00:03:25,087
Height goes as e squared.

86
00:03:25,087 --> 00:03:27,083
One half squared is one quarter.

87
00:03:27,733 --> 00:03:29,422
Now, one worked example.

88
00:03:29,772 --> 00:03:34,062
A two kilogram ball at six metres
per second hits a still one kilogram

89
00:03:34,062 --> 00:03:35,264
ball, head on.

90
00:03:35,264 --> 00:03:36,827
e is zero point five.

91
00:03:36,827 --> 00:03:38,273
Find both velocities.

92
00:03:38,773 --> 00:03:42,357
Momentum: twelve equals
two v one, plus v two.

93
00:03:43,107 --> 00:03:47,977
Restitution: v two minus v one
equals zero point five times six.

94
00:03:47,977 --> 00:03:49,265
That is three.

95
00:03:50,015 --> 00:03:53,921
Put v two equals v one plus
three into the first line.

96
00:03:53,921 --> 00:03:56,603
Three v one plus three equals twelve.

97
00:03:56,603 --> 00:03:59,529
So v one is three, and v two is six.

98
00:04:00,279 --> 00:04:01,525
Check the energy.

99
00:04:01,525 --> 00:04:04,815
Thirty six joules before,
nine plus eighteen after.

100
00:04:04,815 --> 00:04:07,871
Nine joules lost, because
e is less than one.

101
00:04:08,521 --> 00:04:09,982
Now, the exam trap.

102
00:04:10,332 --> 00:04:14,843
Many students take e as one ball's speed
after, divided by its speed before.

103
00:04:15,243 --> 00:04:19,104
That works only when the other
body stays still, like a floor.

104
00:04:19,104 --> 00:04:22,771
In general, use the speed apart
over the speed of approach.

105
00:04:23,421 --> 00:04:24,807
Where is this asked?

106
00:04:24,807 --> 00:04:29,050
In bouncing ball numericals, heights
after many bounces, and two equation

107
00:04:29,050 --> 00:04:30,278
collision problems.

108
00:04:30,878 --> 00:04:32,056
Exam level now.

109
00:04:32,056 --> 00:04:34,472
A ball is dropped from five metres.

110
00:04:34,472 --> 00:04:36,070
e is zero point five.

111
00:04:36,070 --> 00:04:39,924
What total distance does it
travel before it comes to rest?

112
00:04:39,924 --> 00:04:41,153
Pause and solve.

113
00:04:46,553 --> 00:04:47,814
The answer is B.

114
00:04:47,814 --> 00:04:49,867
Eight point three three metres.

115
00:04:50,367 --> 00:04:52,398
The first fall is five metres.

116
00:04:52,398 --> 00:04:56,922
After that, each bounce goes up and
down, and each rise is a quarter of

117
00:04:56,922 --> 00:04:58,058
the one before.

118
00:04:58,558 --> 00:05:03,270
The rises add up: one point two five,
plus a quarter of that, and so on.

119
00:05:03,270 --> 00:05:05,317
That is five thirds of a metre.

120
00:05:05,817 --> 00:05:09,183
Up and down doubles it, to
three point three three.

121
00:05:09,183 --> 00:05:10,567
Add the first five.

122
00:05:10,567 --> 00:05:12,587
Eight point three three metres.

123
00:05:13,187 --> 00:05:14,727
Here is your recap.

124
00:05:14,727 --> 00:05:16,310
Screenshot this card.

125
00:05:19,510 --> 00:05:21,448
Can you answer these without help?

126
00:05:21,848 --> 00:05:22,748
One.

127
00:05:22,241 --> 00:05:24,556
Why does a lump of
dough not bounce at all?

128
00:05:25,456 --> 00:05:26,356
Two.

129
00:05:25,849 --> 00:05:28,550
What does e equal to one
tell you about a collision?

130
00:05:29,450 --> 00:05:30,350
Three.

131
00:05:30,033 --> 00:05:34,629
A ball hits a floor at ten metres
per second, and e is zero point six.

132
00:05:34,629 --> 00:05:36,253
How fast does it leave?

133
00:05:37,153 --> 00:05:40,786
Next time: a carrom striker hits a
coin off centre, and they fly apart

134
00:05:40,786 --> 00:05:41,686
at an angle.

135
00:05:41,601 --> 00:05:43,137
Collisions in two dimensions.
